# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://iauarchive.eso.org/public/themes/constellations/> ,
              <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Main_Page> ,
              <https://asd.gsfc.nasa.gov/archive/ircatalog/constel_names.html> ,
              <https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stj%C3%A4rnbild> ,
              <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IAU_designated_constellations> ,
              <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Constellations> ,
              <https://www.ne.se/uppslagsverk/encyklopedi/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "The list of constellations, their Latin names, abbreviations and English names come from the IAU's page “The Constellations”. Its old address (https://www.iau.org/public/themes/constellations/) returns 404 since the IAU's site was redesigned, and the new iau.org has no constellation page linked from its home page. The page was found in the IAU's own web archive at https://iauarchive.eso.org/public/themes/constellations/ (the reference used by the English Wikipedia article) and downloaded with curl on 2026-10-04; an Internet Archive copy of the old iau.org page from 2023-12-30 was also downloaded and has the identical table. The table under “Charts and tables” was parsed with a short Python script (parse_iau.py, reproduced verbatim under Queries; HTML tags stripped, the download links dropped, the remaining cells read in groups of six: name, pronunciation, abbreviation, English name, genitive, genitive pronunciation). It gives exactly 88 rows."@en ,
                 "Each card's front is the IAU name exactly as the table spells it (e.g. “Boötes” with its diaeresis), tagged as Latin. All 88 are tagged Latin by convention, Dorado included: the IAU names serve as the constellations’ scientific Latin names and are declined as Latin (the IAU table gives the genitive “Doradus”), although “dorado” itself is a Spanish word, as the Dorado card’s note says. The back's English text is the table's “English Name” (described on the page as “the popular name in English”) without its leading “the” (“the Great Bear” → “Great Bear”), with three kinds of exception: (a) Coma Berenices: the table reads “the Bernice's Hair”; the deck spells it “Berenice’s Hair”, as the Latin name itself (Berenices) and Wikipedia's Meaning column (“Berenice's hair”) do. (b) Pyxis: the table reads “the Mariner Compass”; the deck uses the usual English form “Mariner’s Compass” (Wikipedia's Meaning column: “mariner's compass”). (c) Eight constellations named after a figure — Andromeda, Cassiopeia, Cepheus, Eridanus, Hercules, Orion, Pegasus and Perseus — have the figure's own name as their English name, which is what English uses for them; the IAU table gives descriptions for seven of them (“the Chained Maiden”, “the Seated Queen”, “the King”, “the River”, “the Hunter”, “the Winged Horse”, “the Hero”; for Hercules it reads “the Hercules”), and those descriptions are quoted in the card's note instead. Using them as answers would have made the reversed cards ambiguous (“Hero” fits Hercules as well as Perseus) and would not translate the Swedish name on the same card (Cassiopeja, Orion, …)."@en ,
                 "The Swedish names are the Swedish labels (rdfs:label @sv) of the Wikidata items. The items were found with one SPARQL query on https://query.wikidata.org/sparql (listed under Queries) that matches each IAU name against English labels of items that are instances of constellation (Q8928) or a subclass of it and have a short name (P1813); it returned exactly one item for each of the 88 names, and for every item the short name equals the IAU abbreviation. The English and Swedish aliases of the 88 items were fetched with a second query, so that a Wikidata check could also be added where an alias is exactly the card's English text. A first exploratory query (instances of Q8928 only) returned 97 items: it missed Aries and Cancer, which Wikidata types only as zodiac constellation (Q4193029), and included historical or non-IAU constellations (e.g. Sciurus Volans, Marmor Sculptile, Pomum Imperiale); it was used only to see how the data is modelled."@en ,
                 "Swedish cross-check: every Swedish label was compared with the table “Tabell över stjärnbilderna” in the Swedish Wikipedia article “Stjärnbild” (wikitext fetched with https://sv.wikipedia.org/w/api.php?action=parse&page=Stj%C3%A4rnbild&prop=wikitext|revid&format=json&formatversion=2, revision 59706601), parsed with a Python script. That script was not kept; the parsing rules are reproduced by parse_svwiki.py (under Queries), written during review round 2, which gives exactly the same 87 rows from the same saved wikitext (the rowspan rows of Giraffen/Camelopardalis are skipped and that row added by hand, as in the original). All 88 Swedish names and all 88 abbreviations agree. The names follow Swedish convention: definite form of a common noun with a lower-case second word (“Stora björnen”, “Södra korset”), and the Swedish forms of personal names (“Cassiopeja”, “Herkules”)."@en ,
                 "English cross-check: every English name and abbreviation was compared with the “List” table of the English Wikipedia article “IAU designated constellations” (parsed with parse_enwiki.py, under Queries; wikitext fetched with https://en.wikipedia.org/w/api.php?action=parse&page=IAU_designated_constellations&prop=wikitext|revid&format=json&formatversion=2, revision 1375575250). The Meaning column agrees with the IAU's English name for most constellations; where it differs (listed in quality control, round 0) the deck keeps the IAU's name, since the IAU is the authority for the list and the Wikipedia column gives a literal meaning rather than a popular name."@en ,
                 "Abbreviation and list cross-check: the NASA/GSFC page “Three-Letter Constellation Abbreviations” was downloaded with curl and its table parsed; it lists the same 88 constellations with the same abbreviations (in capitals). NASA and Wikidata spell Boötes without the diaeresis in some places (NASA “Bootes”, Wikidata's Latin label “Bootes”); the deck follows the IAU spelling, which is also Wikidata's English label."@en ,
                 "The generator script make_dossier.py (reproduced verbatim under Queries, together with wd_fetch.py and wd_aliases.py, which ran the two Wikidata queries; the raw downloads were kept in a temporary working directory of the authoring agent that is not published) asserts for every card that the IAU abbreviation equals the Wikidata short name, the Swedish Wikipedia abbreviation and the NASA abbreviation, and that the Wikidata Swedish label equals the Swedish Wikipedia name; it then writes each card with its evidence from all five sources. Each card’s note gives the IAU abbreviation and, in the authoring agent’s own words, an explanation where the English and Swedish names clearly differ in meaning (Boötes, Capricornus, Cetus, Corvus, Hydra, Hydrus, Horologium, Pictor, Reticulum, Taurus, Telescopium), where the IAU’s popular name is not used as the answer, or where the name itself needs a word (Dorado); notes on Swedish variants (Cassiopeia, Eridanus, Vela) were added in review round 1. Smaller nuances (e.g. Vela “Sails” / Seglet, singular) get no note."@en ,
                 "Review rounds 1–3 were applied to the dossier by apply_rounds.py and round 4 by apply_round4.py (both reproduced verbatim under Queries). apply_rounds.py reads the saved downloads and review fetches (NE openings, English Wikipedia leads) and edits the dossier that make_dossier.py wrote; replay.py (also under Queries) re-ran it on a saved copy of that pre-review dossier and found the result identical to the published dossier before round 4."@en ,
                 "Swedish names, independent check (added in review rounds 1–2): for every card, Nationalencyklopedin’s article was fetched by the script ne_fetch.py (under Queries) from https://www.ne.se/uppslagsverk/encyklopedi/lång/<Swedish name, lower case, spaces as hyphens>, falling back to <…>-(stjärnbild), and the opening sentence (the free part of the article) kept as evidence. NE gives the card’s Swedish name as the headword for 85 of the 88 cards. The three others: Cassiopeja (NE’s headword is “Cassiopeia”, with “Cassiopeja” as a variant), Eridanus (NE’s main article is “Floden Eridanus”; “Eridanus” is a cross-reference to it) and Seglet (NE calls it “Seglen”, plural). These cards keep the Wikidata and Swedish Wikipedia name, which the builder checks, and give NE’s form in the note."@en ,
                 "English names, second check (added in review round 3): where English Wikipedia’s list renders the IAU’s English name differently, and for the meanings given in the notes, the lead sections of the English Wikipedia articles on those constellations were fetched with the MediaWiki API (under Queries) and the sentence that confirms the name or meaning is quoted in the card’s evidence. The Swedish names of species used in the notes (korp, stenbock, guldmakrill) were looked up as Wikidata labels and are checked at every build."@en ,
                 "Every card has machine checks against Wikidata, run by the builder: the English label equals the IAU Latin name, the Swedish label equals the card's Swedish name, and the short name (P1813) equals the IAU abbreviation; where Wikidata has the card's English text as an English alias, that alias is checked too."@en ,
                 "Study direction is bidirectional: the builder confirms that every English and every Swedish back text is unique in the deck, so the reversed cards (English or Swedish name → Latin name) each have one answer. For 11 constellations the English name is the Latin name (Andromeda, Cassiopeia, Cepheus, Eridanus, Hercules, Lynx, Orion, Pegasus, Perseus, Phoenix, Sculptor), and for six of them the Swedish name is too (Andromeda, Cepheus, Eridanus, Orion, Pegasus, Perseus), so the reversed card gives its answer away; that is inherent to the subject and was accepted."@en ,
                 "Selection: The deck has exactly the 88 constellations in the IAU's table, which are the constellations the IAU recognises (the list agreed in 1922, with boundaries adopted in 1928 and published in 1930); nothing was added and nothing left out. Historical constellations that the IAU does not recognise (Wikidata types some of them as constellations, e.g. Sciurus Volans and Marmor Sculptile) are excluded. Asterisms such as the Big Dipper/Karlavagnen are not constellations and are excluded. Genitive forms, pronunciations, boundaries and star names are left out to keep each card to one fact: the constellation's names."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The deck is licensed CC BY 4.0 because its list of constellations, their Latin names and abbreviations and, above all, their English names (the IAU table's “English Name” column, popular names that are not mere literal translations) are taken from the IAU's page “The Constellations”, whose web texts the IAU releases under CC BY 4.0; the IAU is credited as a source in the deck (prov:wasDerivedFrom, with title, creator and licence) and in this report, and the changes made to its English names (leading “the” dropped, two spellings corrected, eight figure names used instead of descriptions) are listed in the method. Using the IAU page as a content source was a deliberate choice: the CC0 alternatives cover the list and abbreviations (Wikidata; NASA's public-domain table) but not a consistent set of English names (Wikidata's English aliases are incomplete and inconsistent), and CC BY 4.0 lets anyone reuse the deck, commercially too, with attribution. The IAU’s credit conditions are met: the IAU is named, unaltered, in the deck description, in the deck metadata and in the notes that quote its popular names, and nothing in the deck states or implies IAU endorsement. The Swedish names come from Wikidata (CC0), which places no conditions. The NASA page (a US government work), the Swedish Wikipedia article and the English Wikipedia articles (CC BY-SA 4.0), and Nationalencyklopedin (all rights reserved; free quotation only for non-commercial use) were used only to verify facts: no wording, list or selection was taken from them, so their licences put no conditions on the deck. The notes are the authoring agent's own wording."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Sources and machine checks: every card against the IAU table, Wikidata, NASA and both Wikipedias; the builder's Wikidata checks."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 88 cards."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The IAU table, Wikidata (short name P1813), the Swedish Wikipedia table and the NASA table agree on all 88 constellations and all 88 abbreviations; Wikidata's Swedish labels agree with the Swedish Wikipedia table for all 88. Every card has 3 or 4 Wikidata checks (English label = IAU name, Swedish label, P1813 abbreviation, and the English alias where one exactly matches); all pass at build time. The English names were compared with English Wikipedia's Meaning column; the differences are listed below and resolved in favour of the IAU, except two misspellings in the IAU table. Wikidata's Latin labels use classical spellings for three items; they are not used. Limitation: Wikidata's Swedish labels and the Swedish Wikipedia table are maintained by overlapping communities, so they are not fully independent; no third Swedish list (e.g. Nationalencyklopedin, behind a paywall) was consulted."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 88 cards: Latin names, abbreviations and English names against a fresh parse of the IAU table, Wikidata labels and P1813 live, Swedish names against Nationalencyklopedin."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The reviewer found no wrong card: the IAU table matches every Latin name, abbreviation and English name apart from the documented changes, all Wikidata checks pass, and NE confirms the Swedish name of 86 cards (Seglet unconfirmed). One warning (Eridanus: NE’s main Swedish name is “Floden Eridanus”) and five suggestions. The authoring agent re-fetched NE for every card and confirmed the reviewer’s quotes; it also found what the reviewer could not: NE calls Vela “Seglen”, not “Seglet”. All findings were applied except a description change for the trivial reversed cards, which is documented in the method instead."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Language and translation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 88 cards in every language (Latin fronts, English and Swedish backs and notes), plus title, description, keywords and study direction."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The reviewer found no errors and agreed with all 88 Swedish names. Two warnings: five cards whose English and Swedish names differ in meaning lacked the note the method promises, and the Swedish names rested only on Wikidata and Swedish Wikipedia. Three suggestions: idiomatic Swedish on Hydrus, consistent apostrophes, a better Swedish description sentence. All were applied. For the second warning, Nationalencyklopedin was added as a verification source on every card (85 headwords identical, three variants given in notes); in-the-sky.org’s translation table (https://in-the-sky.org/data/constellations_translations.php, downloaded 2026-10-04) was also looked at but not used, because its Swedish column has evident errors (Circinus “Ormen”, Scutum “Sobieskis sköld”)."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 88 cards’ evidence against re-fetched sources; every source’s licence; the method, queries and reproducibility."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The reviewer re-fetched every source and found the licence quotes accurate, the CC BY 4.0 choice right, all evidence matching the sources and no wording copied from closed sources. Two warnings: about ten English answers rested on the IAU table alone, and the scripts that did the work were not published. Four suggestions on the enwiki role text, the IAU’s credit conditions, the NASA licence evidence and grouped round-0 findings. All but the last were applied; the last needs no change."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Final re-check (loop 1): every card changed in the last fix plus every third card, metadata, description, licence and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: 88 cards (every card changed in rounds 1–3 and every third card against the IAU table, live Wikidata, NE and English Wikipedia leads), plus metadata, description, licence and documentation."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The reviewer found no errors or warnings: the IAU table, live Wikidata (labels and P1813), NE openings and English Wikipedia leads agree with the cards, the builder check and the validator pass. Six suggestions; five were applied and one (Dorado’s Latin tag) was answered in the method without changing the card."@en .

<https://iauarchive.eso.org/public/themes/constellations/>
    dcterms:title "The Constellations" ;
    dcterms:creator "International Astronomical Union (IAU)" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "https://iauarchive.eso.org/copyright/ — “The images, videos and web texts on iau.org are released under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International license and may on a non-exclusive basis be reproduced without fee, on the conditions outlined below.” The page is the IAU's former iau.org theme page (https://www.iau.org/public/themes/constellations/, now 404 after the site's redesign), kept in the IAU's web archive hosted at iauarchive.eso.org; the same table and the same copyright text were also seen in the Internet Archive's copies of the iau.org pages (December 2023). The same page sets conditions: “The full image, footage or web text credit must be presented in a clear and readable manner to all users, with the wording unaltered (for example: \"IAU\". The credit should not be hidden or disassociated from the image footage. Links should be active if the credit is online.” and “IAU materials may not be used to state or imply the endorsement by IAU or any IAU employee of a commercial product or service”."@en .

<https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Main_Page>
    dcterms:title "Wikidata" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikidata contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing — “All structured data (i.e. the main, Property, Lexeme, and EntitySchema namespaces) is released into the public domain under Creative Commons Zero.”"@en .

<https://asd.gsfc.nasa.gov/archive/ircatalog/constel_names.html>
    dcterms:title "Three-Letter Constellation Abbreviations" ;
    dcterms:creator "NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, Astrophysics Science Division" ;
    rdfs:comment "https://www.nasa.gov/nasa-brand-center/images-and-media/ — “NASA content – images, audio, video, and media files used in the rendition of 3-dimensional models, such as texture maps and polygon data in any format – generally are not subject to copyright in the United States.” and “NASA content used in a factual manner that does not imply endorsement may be used without needing explicit permission. NASA should be acknowledged as the source of the material.” The page is a work of the US government (NASA/GSFC); it is used here only to verify facts. That guideline speaks of media files; for this text table it is applied by analogy: the table is a work of the US government (17 U.S.C. §105). The page’s own footer link “Privacy Policy & Important Notices” (http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html, which now redirects to https://www.nasa.gov/privacy/) was fetched on 2026-10-04 and contains no copyright statement."@en .

